| visit | new incumbent | vertices | edges |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| 3 | [0, 1, 2, 5] | 4 | 3 |
| 5 | [0, 3, 5] | 3 | 2 |

Final path: [0, 3, 5]; entered prefixes: 9; cycle rejections: 1; bound prunes: 1.
\image(figures/brute_force_incumbents.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The same vertex can be entered through different simple prefixes: one is expanded on the first route and pruned when reached through four later. Edge crossings without labelled circles are not extra vertices.)
\alttext(A directed graph offers a long route zero-one-two-five and two shorter routes zero-three-five and zero-four-five. The route through three is green. An edge from two back to zero forms a cycle, and four can also reach one.)

> The search space and the safe bound

Mark only vertices on the current path. On descent, append a new vertex and
mark it; on return, remove it and clear its mark. Rejecting an arc to a marked
vertex keeps every explored prefix simple. A vertex can still be revisited
through another prefix after backtracking. A shortest path in a unit-cost
graph can always be simple: deleting a repeated-vertex cycle shortens a walk.

Let L be the incumbent's vertex count and let a current prefix have l vertices.
Every extension has at least l vertices. Thus a non-goal prefix with l≥L
cannot lead to a strict improvement and can be pruned. Goals are handled
first: the first goal path or a strictly shorter goal path replaces the
incumbent, while an equal-length goal leaves it unchanged. This retains the
first shortest alternative in the directed adjacency order.

Before the first incumbent, the search explores every permitted prefix. After
finding one, it can omit longer goal paths under pruned prefixes. The original
claim that the implementation enumerates every simple start–goal path is
therefore too strong. Optimality follows instead from the combination of simple
path coverage and the safe length bound: no pruned prefix can contain a better
solution. The bound is tested once on entry to a non-goal prefix; an already
active parent's successor scan continues after a descendant improves the
incumbent, preserving the historical traversal and visit count.

> Actual visit records and the shrinking incumbent

The legacy result field expanded counts entered prefixes, including goals and
bound-pruned visits. It does not count distinct vertices or only visits whose
outgoing arcs are inspected. Each table row captures the actual prefix and
incumbent around its own entry decision. Arc counts accumulate over that
visit's lifetime; descendant improvements do not rewrite its entry fields.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/brute_force_incumbent_length.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges{{0,1},{1,2},{2,0},{2,5},{0,3},{3,5},{0,4},{4,1},{4,5}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brute_force_search_trace(6,edges,0,5);
  const auto fifo=dm::algorithms::breadth_first_search(6,edges,0,5);
  if (!fifo.found || trace.result.path.size()!=fifo.path.size()) return 1;
  std::vector<double> visit,incumbent;std::size_t absent=0;
  std::print("| visit | actual prefix | depth in edges | incumbent vertices before | after own decision | decision | arcs inspected |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& row:trace.steps) {
    const std::string decision=row.improved?"improve":row.goal?"goal, no improvement":row.pruned?"bound prune":"scan successors";
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",row.id,row.path,row.depth,
        row.incumbent_vertices_before,row.incumbent_vertices_after,decision,row.edges_examined);
    if (row.incumbent_vertices_after) {visit.push_back(row.id);incumbent.push_back(row.incumbent_vertices_after);}
    else ++absent;
  }
  const double optimal_vertices=fifo.path.size();
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(visit,incumbent,"incumbent vertices",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(visit,incumbent,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .lines({0,8},{optimal_vertices,optimal_vertices},"optimal vertices (BFS)",dm::RGB{148,163,184},1)
      .title("An incumbent improves from four vertices to three")
      .x_label("actual DFS prefix visit id").y_label("vertices in incumbent path")
      .y_limits(0,6).size(900,480).save_svg("build/brute_force_incumbent_length.svg");
  std::print("\nNo incumbent at {} initial visits; those zero sentinels are omitted from the curve.\n",absent);
  std::print("Entered prefixes: {}; inspected arcs: {}; rejected cycle arcs: {}; goal visits: {}; incumbent changes: {}.\n",
      trace.result.expanded,trace.edges_examined,trace.cycle_rejections,trace.goal_visits,trace.improvements);
}
