\caption(Visit seven is a non-goal bound prune. Visit eight reaches the goal with an equal-length path and leaves the incumbent unchanged. Joined integer visit samples show actual entry decisions, rather than interpolated search events.)
\alttext(The incumbent appears at visit three with four vertices, remains four at visit four, then drops to three at visit five and remains there. A grey line marks the independently computed BFS path's three vertices.)

BFS provides an efficient native reference for the final minimum number of
edges; it is the preferable algorithm when this is the only required result.
Here the backtracking search is useful for inspecting search-space repetition
and incumbent bounds. A full trace copies each live prefix; recording can
omit those path copies while retaining ids, parents, depths and decisions.

> Original chain and boundary controls

The original report's three-vertex chain is retained. It returns [0,1,2],
which has three vertices and two edges, after three entered prefixes. Input
pairs are directed arcs. Reversing the terminals does not insert reverse
arcs. Self-loops are rejected by live-path membership; parallel arcs retain
their original order and can cause repeated visits to the same labelled prefix.
All terminals and arc endpoints are validated before adjacency allocation,
even when start equals goal. An empty graph has no valid terminals.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  struct Example {std::string name;std::size_t n;std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges;std::size_t start,goal;};
  const std::vector<Example> cases{
    {"original chain",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},0,2},{"reverse directed chain",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},2,0},
    {"start equals goal",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},1,1},
    {"parallel arcs and a loop",3,{{0,0},{0,1},{0,1},{1,2}},0,2},
    {"first equal shortest alternative",6,{{0,4},{4,5},{0,3},{3,5}},0,5}};
  std::print("| case | found | path | entered prefixes | inspected arcs | cycle rejections |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& example:cases) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::brute_force_search_trace(example.n,example.edges,example.start,example.goal);
    const auto ordinary=dm::algorithms::brute_force_search(example.n,example.edges,example.start,example.goal);
    if (ordinary.path!=trace.result.path || ordinary.expanded!=trace.result.expanded) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",example.name,trace.result.found,trace.result.path,
        trace.result.expanded,trace.edges_examined,trace.cycle_rejections);
  }
  try {
    dm::algorithms::brute_force_search(3,{{0,3}},0,0);return 1;
  } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
    std::print("\nAn invalid arc is rejected even for a start-equals-goal call.\n");
  }
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges{{0,1},{1,2},{2,0},{2,5},{0,3},{3,5},{0,4},{4,1},{4,5}};
  try {
    dm::algorithms::brute_force_search_trace(6,edges,0,5,{4,false});return 1;
  } catch (const std::runtime_error&) {
    std::print("A four-visit budget stops after the long incumbent, without returning it as an optimum.\n");
  }
}
```
