\caption(At k=9, DFS enters 109,601 prefixes and inspects 876,808 arcs, while BFS dequeues nine vertices. The ordinate is explicitly log10 of positive measured counts, with no artificial floor.)
\alttext(The logarithm of DFS prefix visits accelerates upwards to about five at component size nine. The logarithm of BFS dequeues grows slowly to just below one.)

The explicit stack has at most n live frames, and it avoids native recursion
depth limits. Ordinary search uses O(n+m) live storage. With V entered prefixes,
A inspected arcs and I vertex ids copied into improving incumbents, its work
is O(n+m+V+A+I). A trace adds O(V) record storage and, by default, the total
length of copied prefixes. Disabling those copies preserves every other field.
The factorial argument above assumes unique arcs; duplicate arcs can repeat
equivalent prefixes and add more work.

The search maintains only a unit-edge length objective. It does not solve a
weighted shortest-path problem, and the bound is too weak to prevent factorial
work when no incumbent is available. These limitations explain why the example
is a study of exhaustive search with bounding rather than a replacement for BFS.

> References and verification

[NIST's Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/exhaustiveSearch.html)
uses exhaustive search for trying every possibility. This library's existing
brute_force_search name denotes the more specific incumbent-bound path-prefix
method described here. A. H. Land and A. G. Doig (1960), *An Automatic Method
of Solving Discrete Programming Problems*, Econometrica 28(3), 497–520,
[doi:10.2307/1910129](https://doi.org/10.2307/1910129), is general branch-and-bound
background; the simple graph bound here is derived directly from prefix length.

The original chain and worked graph are fully specified; the dense family is
generated in the core without a dataset or random seed. Native tests compare
all directed graphs through four vertices and every terminal pair with independent
Floyd–Warshall distances and first-adjacency shortest-path recovery. They also
check actual incumbent history, counters, ordered loops/duplicates, the factorial
family, complete-call limits, invalid inputs and a 50,000-vertex chain with path
snapshots omitted. Every report block is an editable complete C++ programme;
figures are local SVG files rather than XML printed into the results.
